            <h2>Temple silhouettes — Washington County's defining motif</h2>
            <p>No other county in Utah has two operating LDS temples within its borders. That fact shapes the memorial culture here in a way no outside monument company fully understands.</p>

            <h3>St. George Utah Temple (dedicated 1877)</h3>
            <p>The St. George Utah Temple is the oldest continuously operating temple in the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Dedicated by Brigham Young on April 6, 1877, it was built by Cotton Mission pioneers from locally quarried red sandstone and white stucco — a white castellated tower rising from the desert floor that has served as the spiritual landmark of Washington County for nearly 150 years.</p>
            <p>On headstones, the St. George Temple silhouette is almost always rendered as a white sandblasted or laser-etched outline on polished black or dark gray granite — the high contrast makes the distinctive towers and spires read cleanly at a distance. Families who were endowed here, married here, or whose parents and grandparents were married here choose this motif as the central expression of their faith and their place.</p>
            <p>This is our most-requested design. It appears on uprights, flat markers, companion stones, and bench memorials across every Washington County cemetery. <a href="/headstones/st-george-temple-headstones" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">See our St. George Temple engraving gallery.</a></p>

            <h3>Red Cliffs Utah Temple (dedicated 2024)</h3>
            <p>The Red Cliffs Utah Temple was dedicated in November 2024 — the second temple in Washington County and the first modern temple built specifically for a community whose identity is rooted in the Cotton Mission pioneer era. Its design features a contemporary form with a distinctive cottonwood-leaf motif on the exterior, reflecting the riparian landscape of the Virgin River corridor.</p>
            <p>On headstones, the Red Cliffs Temple silhouette is newer and still being established as a cultural marker — but it is already appearing regularly, particularly for younger Washington County families endowed after 2024. Companion monuments combining both the St. George Temple (for older family members) and the Red Cliffs Temple (for the next generation) on a single stone are becoming one of our most-requested designs. <a href="/headstones/red-cliffs-temple-headstones" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">See our Red Cliffs Temple engraving gallery.</a></p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>Pioneer heritage symbols</h2>

            <h3>Sego Lily (Utah state flower)</h3>
            <p>The sego lily is Utah's state flower — chosen because the bulbs of the plant sustained the early Mormon pioneers through starvation winters when crops failed. In scriptural symbolism, the lily represents purity, virtue, and resurrection. On headstones, the sego lily usually appears as a delicate engraved motif alongside names and dates, or as part of a border design. It is particularly common in Washington County, where the Cotton Mission pioneers who settled here were the same generation who depended on the sego lily in their first winters in the Salt Lake Valley.</p>

            <h3>Beehive (Deseret)</h3>
            <p>The beehive is one of the most recognized LDS symbols — and Utah's state symbol — representing industry, cooperation, and the community of the Saints. The word "Deseret" in the Book of Mormon (Ether 2:3) is translated as "honeybee." Early Utah was called the State of Deseret before statehood. The beehive on a headstone speaks to a life of service and hard work within the community, virtues central to the Cotton Mission settlers who built Southern Utah.</p>

            <h3>California Sea Gull (Utah state bird)</h3>
            <p>In 1848, a plague of crickets threatened to destroy the first Mormon crop in the Salt Lake Valley. A flock of California sea gulls arrived and consumed the crickets, saving the harvest — an event the early Saints interpreted as a miracle. The sea gull became Utah's state bird and a symbol of divine intervention and preservation. On headstones it appears less frequently than the temple or beehive, but families with deep pioneer roots sometimes include it as a statement of faith in providence.</p>

            <h3>Handcart</h3>
            <p>The handcart companies of 1856–1860 carried more than 3,000 Latter-day Saint converts from Iowa City to Salt Lake City, pulling their belongings in wooden carts after insufficient funds prevented them from buying wagons. The Willie and Martin handcart companies suffered devastating losses in an early Rocky Mountain winter — nearly 200 deaths among the last companies to cross. The handcart symbol on a headstone typically marks descent from one of these companies, or more broadly, from pioneer immigrant converts. In Washington County, whose early settlers were called south from Salt Lake just a few years after the handcart era, handcart heritage is part of the founding identity.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>Religious and theological symbols</h2>

            <h3>Angel Moroni</h3>
            <p>The Angel Moroni — typically depicted as a standing figure with a trumpet raised — is not an official symbol of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, but it appears widely on LDS headstones. Moroni was the last prophet in the Book of Mormon, who buried the golden plates in the Hill Cumorah and later appeared to Joseph Smith as a resurrected messenger. His image on a headstone speaks to the Restoration and the promise of resurrection. The figure with a trumpet raised is associated with the verse in Revelation 14:6 — "an angel fly in the midst of heaven, having the everlasting gospel to preach."</p>

            <h3>CTR Shield</h3>
            <p>The "Choose the Right" shield — a distinctive octagonal green badge familiar to anyone who has attended Primary in the Church — appears occasionally on children's and young adults' headstones. It is a straightforward expression of faith formation, most common on infant, child, and youth memorials. In the Tonaquint Angel section and similar dedicated sections at other Washington County cemeteries, the CTR shield appears regularly.</p>

            <h3>Eternal Flame</h3>
            <p>A flame — typically depicted as a simple upward-reaching fire image — represents the eternal nature of the soul and the ongoing presence of the deceased in the family's life. It is an interdenominational symbol used across many faiths, but in LDS context it often connects to the concept of the Light of Christ and the eternal nature of the family covenant.</p>

            <h3>clasped hands</h3>
            <p>Two hands clasped in a handshake — a common Victorian-era motif that persists on LDS headstones today — represents the reunion of souls after death. In LDS theology, the family is eternal: "families can be together forever." Clasped hands on a companion monument speak directly to the sealing covenant and the anticipated reunion. They appear most often on older upright monuments in the pioneer sections of St. George City Cemetery and Santa Clara Cemetery.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>Choosing symbols for a Southern Utah headstone</h2>
            <p>Most Washington County families combine two or three motifs rather than a single large symbol. The most common combinations we design:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Temple silhouette + sego lily border</strong> — the temple centered or offset, sego lilies as a decorative band across the bottom of the stone. Classic Southern Utah.</li>
              <li><strong>Both temples on a companion stone</strong> — St. George Temple on one side panel, Red Cliffs Temple on the other, with names and dates between them. For families spanning the 2024 dedication.</li>
              <li><strong>Beehive + handcart</strong> — for families with documented pioneer handcart heritage. Common in the oldest sections of Washington City Cemetery and St. George City Cemetery.</li>
              <li><strong>Temple + CTR shield</strong> — on a child's or young adult's memorial, combining the adult covenant symbol with the childhood faith formation symbol.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>There are no rules about which symbols are "correct" for a given family — the design should reflect the specific person, their generation, and their relationship to their faith. We help families think through these choices without prescribing an outcome.</p>
